      "writtenOn": "2026-08-03T23:17:14Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yes, this is intentional and `unmet_requirement:foo` would match all changes where `foo` timed out (or errored).\n\n1. **Behavior on ERROR or TIMEOUT:** Both `UNMET_REQUIREMENT_FIELD` (`unmet_requirement:`) and `UNSATISFIED_REQUIREMENT_COUNT_FIELD` (`unsatisfied_requirement_count:`) filter requirements by `!sr.fulfilled()`. Since `SubmitRequirementResult#fulfilled()` returns `true` only for `SATISFIED`, `OVERRIDDEN`, `NOT_APPLICABLE`, or `FORCED`, any evaluation resulting in `ERROR` or `TIMEOUT` is considered unfulfilled (`fulfilled() \u003d\u003d false`). Its name is included in `UNMET_REQUIREMENT_FIELD` and it is counted in `UNSATISFIED_REQUIREMENT_COUNT_FIELD`.\n\n2. **Would `unmet_requirement:foo` match all changes on timeout?:** Yes. Because `TIMEOUT` is considered unfulfilled (`!sr.fulfilled()`), if requirement `foo` times out during evaluation when a change is indexed, `\"foo\"` is added to `UNMET_REQUIREMENT_FIELD`. Therefore, querying `unmet_requirement:foo` matches all changes where `foo` timed out (or errored or was unsatisfied).",
      "parentUuid": "27322e2b_1244f09f",
      "revId": "527652179a64b4cec32c6641cfa287de33f53a76",
      "serverId": "173816e5-2b9a-37c3-8a2e-48639d4f1153"
    },
